# THE FUSIONS THAT CALL THE DOUBLE-COUNTING QUESTION MALFORMED, WITH THE PARTS THEY ACTUALLY
# HAVE. One row per composed layer filing its elimination search as `notApplicable`, carrying
# how many parts it names.
#
# ⭐⭐ BETWEEN A SET OF ONE, NOTHING CAN BE COUNTED TWICE -- so `notApplicable` is exactly
#    right on a single-part fusion and the question genuinely does not arise. Between two or
#    more it DOES arise: `notApplicable` there is a claim that two supplies cannot possibly
#    overlap, which is a judgement about the world and belongs in `observed` where somebody
#    signs it.
#
# ⛔ THE PART COUNT IS THE WHOLE POPULATION TEST. A `notApplicable` search on a fusion with no
#   parts filed at all is not examined by the rule and must not be counted as passing it,
#   which is why the count comes from a join rather than from the search row alone.
-- eliminations/searched.sqlc answering "notApplicable", counted over asrt:part.
SELECT es.composition, es.composed_layer, count(*) AS parts
FROM (
    :compose(eliminations/searched.sqlc)
) es
JOIN (
    :compose(composition/part_references.sqlc)
) p
  ON p.composition = es.composition AND p.composed_layer = es.composed_layer
WHERE es.answer = 'notApplicable'
GROUP BY es.composition, es.composed_layer
